# Gate review — M-next D11, the fenced writer (read-only)

Reviewed the whole uncommitted diff in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d11` (branch `ucf-d11-writer`, on 441b2b4c): `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer.cljc` (new, 449 lines), `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` (new, 954 lines, 19 deftests), `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` (57+/11−). Against r3 1.3/1.11 and the D11 test contract, the D10 inputs ruling's custody shape, UCF 7.7.5/7.7.8, and the front/admission/ledger code the writer composes with. I audited the diff as a whole, not just the continuation delta (the continuation round added no edits and no evidence beyond JVM re-runs; its "perfectly matches" is an overclaim of scope, and everything it asserts checks out against the sources).

## Verdict: **READY**

One landing condition (evidence, not code) and one optional one-word hardening below. No contract breach found in the code.

## The attack list, point by point

**1. Assign-before-send as one atomic step — holds.**
`assign` (writer.cljc:169–195) is one pure pass over the machine value before any appender call: every `:put` entry whose class is `:enrolled` and which carries no `:op-id` takes `n` from `get-in m [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/next-op-seq]` — always the **root's** custody, so children draw from the one counter (writer.cljc:184; children carry no custody map per D10) — stores `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` and sets the counter to `n+1` in the same threaded value (187–193). Entries that already carry an id are skipped (180–181), which makes re-emit idempotent and preserves a lower's carried ids (D10 ruling §4: "preserve it; do not assign a replacement"). At `custody/max-exact` (2^52−1, custody.cljc:24–26) nothing is assigned (185–186) and `emit`'s arm for an id-less enrolled write sends nothing (writer.cljc:286–289). The send arm is only reached when `(:op-id entry)` is some — no path sends without an id. The counter increments exactly once per write (one reduce step per entry), and the second-emit test pins no second increment and byte-identical re-request (test 324–333). The equality-indexed `put-entry` (118–127) is safe against duplicate entries because real parked `:put` entries always differ by `:yin.k/issue` (engine.cljc:2608–2612; the v1 lower issues them too, handoff.cljc:1901–1908).

**2. The envelope and request id — hold.**
`envelope` (134–143) is exactly the five-key 7.7.8 grammar (`:yin.k/envelope :yin.k/fenced-v1`, incarnation = the custody lease, epoch from the grant binding, the id, the program value verbatim as `:datom`); `request-id` (146–152) is the tagged vector `[:yin.k/admit lease op-id]`; `admit-request` (155–162) matches the front's closed admit shape (`:yin.k/target`, `:yin.k/fenced-envelope`, front.cljc:44–48, 121). No envelope key ever reaches machine state: the entry gains only `:op-id` — proven by `a-real-writers-entries-equal-the-d9-fixtures-helper-test` (380–399), where a real `emit` run's wait entry *equals* `s/with-op-id`'s and both machines lift to the same canonical address (the lift strips custody/gate, handoff.cljc:1779, so the fixture non-regeneration claim is sound; the `:op-id` ↔ pending `:yin.k/op-id` round trip is handoff.cljc:586 and 1903–1904). The writer never treats a reply's request id as dedup: it is only a match field in `discharge-reply` (363), and resends re-append the same request (test 331–333) — the authority's op-id namespace is the dedup.

**3. Retention and discharge — hold.**
Retention is structural: enrolled sends never mutate the machine (292–296), so the id survives `full` (test 406–422 — note `read-all` drains the one-slot stream, so the retry's `ok` is real), unknown-effect (445–462), no outcome yet (465–476), and `:suspended` (479–488). Discharge authenticates through `front/reply-evidence` (414–417; identity equality only, front.cljc:331–345), then the five fields each refuse independently in `discharge-reply` (348–374): kind (355), rid shape (357), the entry's *current* request id (363 — this is also what retires a pre-regrant reply's old lease), op id (366), incarnation (369); all five mutations are tested one at a time (495–524). The projected arm (377–388) matches on op id and incarnation only; a committed projection of another incarnation is ignored with `:incarnation` retained (527–548). A duplicate outcome for a discharged wait finds no entry and skips as `:no-entry` (308–315; exercised end-to-end in 613–637 where the second `:replayed` reply is retained while the first `:committed` discharged). `settle` (323–345) applies `:committed`/`:replayed` through `apply-put`/`apply-ffi-outcome` with the recorded `:yin.k/effect-result` (a DaoStream outcome map unchanged — `ledger/ok-result` ledger.cljc:149), ends the run on `:intent-conflict`/`:stale`, retains on `:suspended` and on any unknown admission keyword.

**4. `:intent-conflict` aftermath — holds.**
`ended` (202–209) stamps `:yin.k/gate :ended` on the root and every install child; `emit` is a no-op for a non-running machine before any class walk or append (252–253), so no further fenced emission *and* no at-least-once write (test 771–777: appender call count stays 1, the bare stream unchanged). The conflicting entry itself is never applied (765–767). The release goes through the real front and answers `:carried` (778–786); the judge lapses the lease; quarantine survives the release, the occurrence stays open (no `:yin.k/closed`) and ungranted — a fresh proposal yields no second grant and the ledger still holds exactly one `:dao.lease/accepted` (787–803). This matches residual 2's quarantine rule exactly.

**5. Protection classes — hold.**
Every write passes the cond (266–296): undeclared → `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the identity and the walk stops (fail closed; test 708–720); `:fail-stop` → the run ends *before* performing it, root and children stamped, no id taken, counter untouched (678–705, per the D10 ruling "D11 ends the run before performing it"); `:at-least-once` → no id, appended bare, outcome applied at once, `full` keeps it waiting (644–675); enrolled never goes bare — its only arm is the appender.

**6. Carried obligations — hold.**
`refuse-unminted!` (engine.cljc:698–706) guards both `apply-next` (1949) and `apply-observation` (723); the test drives the real gate (`handle-cursor-unminted`), shows both applies throw with zero stream calls, and mint-then-apply advancing normally (860–900). `apply-ffi-outcome` (2000–2018) is exactly the ungated sweep's `poll-put` disposition (dao/stream/waitset.cljc:133–161) and `apply-put`'s shape (1908–1931): invalid answer → `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`, `full` → keep waiting, `ok` → the `apply-ffi-sent` transition (equality asserted, 922–930), terminal → woken under its own status; refused as `apply-ffi-sent` is, including the second-transition refusal (941–954). The `ffi-request-slot` extraction is behavior-identical to the old inline code (`require-running!` + the same keep-indexed/fail).

**7. The unknown-effect cuts — hold.**
Before the commit: the dropping appender leaves the request un-landed, the id retained, and the retry commits once (599–610). After the commit: the cutting appender lands the request and steps the front (so the authority commits) but answers `transport-error`; the retry's own reply is `:replayed` with the recorded result, one effect in the target, the wait discharged (613–637). This is 7.7.8's "Results and uncertainty" rule implemented as one structural rule — retain and resend through the fence.

**8. Scope, portability, style — hold.**
`git status`/diff confirm exactly the three named files (`handoff.cljc` untouched; nothing depends on `writer` yet). The custody keys the writer reads (`:protection`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/arbitration`) are precisely what the D10 lower sets (handoff.cljc:1738–1768). No transport vocabulary: the inbound appender and outcome reader are parameters; `stream/append!` appears only on the machine's own resource handle in the at-least-once arm — a disclosed scope decision consistent with r3 1.11 ("the driver appends bare") and flagged as a one-line change if the architect wants it behind a supplied function. Host determinism: children walked in `sort-by str` install-name order (74), only exact-integer compares against `custody/max-exact` (no float traps, no `0.0` literals), and the single reader conditional (test:44) carries all three host branches, so the `:cljd`-first rule for JVM-only branches has nothing to bite on. kondo/cljstyle were the orchestrator's, per the task statement; the sources read clean to the repo's conventions by eye.

## Numbered findings

1. **The full Dart lane was never recorded (landing condition).** The engineer's own report leaves the full-lane line as "**[filled below]**" (findings line 137) and names it "the one open leg" (209–213); the recorded Dart evidence is the two targeted namespaces (writer +19, engine-gate +55, both green). The continuation round re-ran JVM only. r3 requires three lanes once at each landing, so the full Dart lane must be run and recorded — by the orchestrator at gate or before the commit — before this lands. No code change is implied; the two targeted Dart runs and the portability audit above give no reason to expect a failure.
2. **Minor, fail-open edge in `emit`'s dispatch (writer.cljc:266–296).** The cond arms cover nil/`:fail-stop`/`:at-least-once`/id-less and fall through to `:else` = the enrolled send. A class value outside the three (only reachable through a hand-built custody map — D10's lower throws on malformed classes, handoff.cljc:1740–1743) would be silently fenced rather than refused. An explicit `(= :enrolled class)` arm (else unsatisfied/refused) would fail closed for one word. Not blocking: unreachable through the sanctioned path.
3. **Note: a throwing `append-request!` propagates out of `emit` (writer.cljc:293).** The contract has the appender answer outcome maps (dao.stream appends are total over outcomes; the front catches its own throws, front.cljc:174–181), so a throw is an implementation fault, not a transport condition — but D13/D15 wiring should keep that invariant, or `emit` needs a guard.
4. **Disclosed scope decisions, audited and accepted** (all three were reported by the engineer): the at-least-once bare append through the machine's own resource handle; `assign` running before the class walk, so writes that will never be sent (behind a fail-stop or an unsatisfied target) still consume counter values — inert on an ended run (export of an ended machine is refused) and deterministic on replay; link-request writes left to D13's `apply-link-cursor`/`apply-link-sent` choreography, which the D11 test contract's rows do not touch.

## Contract-row coverage (r3's D11 test contract)

All seven rows have real-machine tests through the real authority, front and judge: assign before send / one increment / children draw from the root (299–373); retention across the four cases (406–488); each of the five match fields refusing alone (495–524); a projected outcome of another incarnation ignored (527–548); regrant → new request id, same op id, new-epoch envelope, replay of the held result (555–592); the cuts before and after the commit (599–637); and the `:intent-conflict` aftermath with quarantine surviving the release (727–803). The three carried obligations each have their own test (380–399, 860–900, 903–954).

**The diff may land once finding 1's Dart full-lane record exists; finding 2's one-word guard is recommended but optional.**
